### Integration Test for Kafka Event Sourcing
### This file contains HTTP requests to test the end-to-end event flow

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5196
@productId = 493c0e1d-ba57-4981-b0fe-a614c03f552e

### 1. Create a Product (should publish ProductCreatedEvent to Kafka)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/products/CreateProduct
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "createProductDto": {
    "userId": 1,
    "name": "Test Product",
    "price": 99.99,
    "stock": 100
  }
}

### 2. Change Product Name (should publish ProductNameChangedEvent to Kafka)
# Replace @productId with actual product ID from database after step 1
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/products/ChangeName
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "changeProductNameDto": {
    "id": "{{productId}}",
    "name": "Updated Product Name"
  }
}

### 3. Change Product Price (should publish ProductPriceChangedEvent to Kafka)
# Replace @productId with actual product ID from database after step 1
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/products/ChangePrice
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "changeProductPriceDto": {
    "id": "{{productId}}",
    "price": 149.99
  }
}

### 4. Delete Product (should publish ProductDeletedEvent to Kafka)
# Replace @productId with actual product ID from database after step 1
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/products/DeleteProduct/{{productId}}

###
### Expected Flow:
### 1. Command Handler receives command
### 2. ProductStream publishes event to Kafka topic
### 3. EventConsumerService consumes event from Kafka
### 4. EventConsumerService updates SQL Server database
### 5. Query returns updated data
###
### To verify:
### - Check application logs for "Message published to Kafka"
### - Check application logs for "The Message processing..."
### - Check Kafka UI at http://localhost:8080 to see messages in product-events topic
### - Verify database changes using SQL Server Management Studio
###
